\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The degree-two symbol \citet{II.1.6, pp. 41--42}{chevalley1954algebraic}}
\lean/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.filtrationLeadingTerm_apply_ιMulti}

\p{Let #{R}, #{M}, and #{Q} be as in \ref{fcap-000G}, and assume #{2} is invertible in #{R}, as in \ref{fcap-000H}. For #{u,v\in M}, the degree-two leading-term map sends their exterior product to the class of the corresponding Clifford word. Writing that word as

##{\iota(u)\iota(v)
=\frac12\bigl(\iota(u)\iota(v)-\iota(v)\iota(u)\bigr)
+\frac12B_Q(u,v)1.}

The second summand belongs to #{F_0}, so it vanishes in #{F_2/F_1}. Thus #{u\wedge v} maps to the class of #{\iota(u)\iota(v)}; equivalently, under the graded-piece equivalence, that class maps to #{u\wedge v}. This is the two-generator instance of Chevalley's leading-symbol calculation cited in the title. The surviving half-commutator is the Clifford bivector studied next; the scalar polar term is invisible to the leading symbol.}
